The coverage gate, hit for real on the source it was written for.

Reproduction (no re-transcription and no re-extraction — a resumed run, the shape
of an editor re-running a finished project):

    OUT=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p "$OUT/project/transcription"
    cp corpus/telugu-tutorial_run/project/transcript.json       "$OUT/project/"
    cp corpus/telugu-tutorial_run/project/transcription/audio.wav "$OUT/project/transcription/"
    SRC=$(realpath corpus/media/telugu-tutorial.webm)
    .venv/bin/python - "$OUT" "$SRC" <<'PY'
    import json, sys
    from pathlib import Path
    sys.path.insert(0, ".")
    from selekyt.pipeline import _fingerprint
    out, src = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
    media = Path(src).expanduser()
    Path(out, "project", "pipeline_state.json").write_text(json.dumps({
        "source": src, "media_path": str(media), "media_info": {},
        "fingerprint": _fingerprint(media, src, "small", None),
        "model": "small", "language": "te",
    }), encoding="utf-8")
    PY

    # 1. The gate aborts on the real source.
    .venv/bin/selekyt "$SRC" --out "$OUT" --limit 3

    # 2. Bypassing it does not reach a deliverable: the editorial confidence
    #    gate refuses one stage later and names the transcription as the suspect.
    .venv/bin/selekyt "$SRC" --out "$OUT" --limit 3 --skip-coverage-check

Both outputs follow, verbatim. The numbers are the corpus survey's
(corpus/wer/runs/coverage-2026-09-27.json): 4.5% transcript coverage against
77.4% speech scored by silero over 339.6 s, ratio 0.058.
